# Coverage config for the cross-platform test matrix ONLY.
#
# Those runners have no Redis, so every test under tests/live/ skips and
# microguard/live can never execute there. Including it in the measurement does
# not gate that code -- it only drags the reported number down as the live path
# grows, which is exactly what happened: 93% on a branch measured at 100% by a
# job that can actually run the tests.
#
# microguard/live is gated in the `live (Redis-backed)` job instead, which has a
# Redis service container. Between the two jobs, every line is still covered by
# a gate. Local runs use pyproject.toml and measure everything.
#
# The MLflow files are the same situation with one honest difference. This job
# installs .[live,fastapi,flask,dashboard] and NOT the mlflow extra, so every
# function in tracking.py but resolved_tracking_uri() -- and all of
# api_mlflow.py -- raises ImportError before doing anything. Measuring them
# here gated nothing and cost 35 missed statements: the total read 97.74%,
# which only passed --cov-fail-under=98 because coverage.py rounds to the
# display precision before comparing. Eight more uncovered statements anywhere
# would have turned it red, with the real cause three files away.
#
# UNLIKE microguard/live, there is NO second job that installs the mlflow extra,
# so these two files are currently UNGATED rather than gated elsewhere. That is
# a known gap, not an oversight: closing it means adding `mlflow` to one job's
# install line. Until then, do not read this omit as "covered somewhere else".
[run]
source = microguard
omit =
    microguard/vulture_whitelist.py
    microguard/live/*
    microguard/tracking.py
    microguard/dashboard/api_mlflow.py

[report]
exclude_lines =
    pragma: no cover
    if __name__ == .__main__.:
    ^\s*\.\.\.$
